"Every academic mutation is recorded in an append-only trail. An interceptor is applied only to routes that opt in, so read paths incur no cost. For each mutation the trail stores the actor, the action, the entity changed and its identifier, a structured payload including a written reason where the operation warrants one, and the request context — the originating address, the client fingerprint, the method and the route.\n\nThe action vocabulary is fixed and filterable, and covers creation, update, deletion, bulk import, confirmation, correction, grant, revocation, deactivation and archiving. Corrections are given their own action rather than being filed as an ordinary update, because a correction overwrites work that another person is responsible for; privilege changes are likewise given their own actions, since they are the most consequential thing the system records. Beyond the audited routes, the system records logins, password changes, profile updates, avatar changes, invitation acceptances, password-reset requests, role grants and revocations, and member deactivations.\n\nTwo rules govern the write itself. Only successful mutations are recorded, since a handler that threw changed nothing. And a failed audit write never fails the request, because the mutation has already committed and aborting the response afterwards would be worse than a missing row; the failure is logged and the row dropped. The write is nonetheless awaited before the response returns, so a shutdown cannot lose committed rows.",
